index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ced6805b829165463a03c095c5243628ec73bab4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+# Planted 2-metric generators
+
+**Current status: locally verified, but not shippable.** The mandatory OpenRouter
+oracle run was attempted twice and stopped before a deciding answer because the
+provided key returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` across vendors.
+G9 and STEP 4 therefore remain unproved; `selftest_report.json` marks them failed
+rather than counting API errors as model failures.
+
+| profile axis | declaration |
+|---|---|
+| track | `A` — structural hardness |
+| native domain | `combinatorics` |
+| object regime | `finite_discrete` |
+| computational core | `graph` |
+| certificate form | `integer_tuple` (a vertex subset) |
+| intended intuition | `constraint propagation`: distance signatures become adjacency-code columns in diameter two |
+| domain essentiality | `native` |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust basis
+
+This generator turns Estrada-Moreno, Rodríguez-Velázquez, and Yero's
+[*The k-metric dimension of a graph*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6840) into a
+native graph search problem. The solver receives a simple connected graph as an
+exact adjacency matrix and must return exactly `r` vertices such that every pair
+of graph vertices has different distances to at least two returned vertices.
+The checker runs breadth-first search and counts those distinguishers exactly;
+it accepts any valid set and never reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse. It chooses the landmark set first, gives every other
+vertex a random landmark-adjacency word, and conditions the words to have mutual
+Hamming distance at least two and to differ appropriately from every landmark
+row. Random remaining edges are accepted only when the graph has diameter two.
+The adjacency words are then the exact distance words, so the planted witness is
+known without solving the generated instance.
+
+Section 1 fixes the definition. Theorem 2.2 is the important triage result: the
+largest feasible `k` is `min |D_G(x,y)|`, so that different question has a direct
+polynomial scan and was not used. The easy classes were also avoided: Corollary
+2.3 handles twins, Proposition 2.7 and Theorem 2.8 handle cycles and paths, and
+Theorems 4.1 and 4.3 give direct formulas and witnesses for trees. Generated
+graphs are dense, diameter two, twin-free, and not in those classes.
+
+The original paper does not prove computational hardness. Track A instead uses
+Schmitz, Vietz, and Wanke, *A note on the complexity of k-Metric Dimension*
+(2023), Theorem 2: `k`-Metric Dimension is NP-complete for every fixed `k >= 2`,
+even on bipartite graphs. This module uses fixed `k=2`; its graphs are general
+dense graphs. That is only worst-case support, not a theorem about this planted
+distribution. Distribution-specific evidence is the attack panel: all six
+attacks failed on 8/8 shipping instances, while exact multicover branching hit
+its 100,000-node cap.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This smallest preset is genuinely hand-scale: there are only 70 four-subsets,
+and the adjacency rows can be compared directly.
+
+```text
+Fault-tolerant resolving landmarks (2-metric generator)
+
+Vertices are 0 through 7. Find exactly 4 distinct vertices so every unordered
+pair is distinguished by unequal graph distance to at least 2 selected vertices.
+
+0: 00100110
+1: 00100101
+2: 11011000
+3: 00101010
+4: 00110011
+5: 11000011
+6: 10011101
+7: 01001110
+
+Return a strictly increasing JSON array inside <answer></answer> tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>[0, 3, 6, 7]</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, [0, 3, 6, 7])` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping the last
+entry returns `(False, "wrong number of landmarks: expected 4, got 3")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | vertices `n` | answer length `r` | edge density | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| `demo` | 8 | 4 | 50% | hand example only |
+| `easy` | 40 | 8 | 44% | proposed shipping level; local gates pass |
+| `medium` | 72 | 10 | 48% | local construction verified; oracle not reached |
+| `hard` | 128 | 12 | 50% | local construction verified; oracle not reached |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` currently names `easy`, but this is provisional until
+the bare and hinted oracle pools both return `hardened`.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verified (4 presets × 3 seeds) |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | realistic prose + fenced answer round-tripped all 8 indices |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 uniform exact-size subset guesses; space `C(40,8)=76,904,685` |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; exact baseline unsolved at 100,000 nodes in 0.686407 s |
+| G6 | 0/8 for lexicographic, outlier, greedy, 256-restart, spectral, and exact attacks |
+| G7 | 80-vertex doubled instance verified; space grew to 1,646,492,110,120 |
+| G8 | 20/20 relabellings invariant, 20/20 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **blocked/failed**: answer is 23 chars, 8 atoms, ≈6 tokens; intended route 24 mask operations, but no oracle attempt completed |
+
+The exact demo solution counts for seeds 5, 6, and 7 were respectively 8, 3,
+and 7 out of 70. Those readable instances are intentionally guessable; they do
+not substitute for the shipping density measurement.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+No row below is a deciding attempt. Every call is recorded with
+`solved="error"`; the harness correctly aborted instead of manufacturing a
+hardness result.
+
+| arm | requested preset | completed decisions | observed error |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | `easy` | 0/3 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded (Gemini, Terra) |
+| structural hint | `easy` | 0/3 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded (Grok, Gemini) |
+| placebo hint | `easy` | 0/3 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded (Terra, Claude) |
+
+The G9 arms are therefore bare `0/0`, hinted `0/0`, placebo `0/0`; hinted minus
+placebo is undefined. Nothing can yet be concluded about whether the structural
+hint helps. The error-only transcripts are retained as evidence of the failed
+runs, not as evidence for hardness.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_1312_6840 as gen
+
+params = gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = gen.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
+question = gen.render(inst)
+candidate = gen.parse_answer("<answer>[...]</answer>")
+ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+After restoring OpenRouter quota, rerun from this directory:
+
+```bash
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_1312_6840.py
+bash ../../scripts/emit.sh 1312.6840 20 easy
+```
+
+The hinted and placebo runs must each use their own scratch directory because
+`harden.py` overwrites its transcript.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- NP-completeness is worst-case and does not prove the planted distribution
+  hard. Until the required multi-vendor loop completes, the family must not ship.
+- The observed 0/200,000 rate is for a uniform sorted 8-subset. It does not prove
+  zero density or model guided search; a zero-hit 95% upper bound is about
+  `1.5e-5`.
+- The exact attack is capped. It proves only that this implementation explored
+  100,000 nodes without success, not that an unrestricted ILP, SAT encoding, or
+  commercial solver would take long. Those stronger solvers were not run.
+- The spectral panel uses centered-adjacency power iteration, not an SDP or
+  higher-order tensor method. A construction-aware algebraic attack may exist.
+- Canonicalization is exact only because generation rejects graphs whose 1-WL
+  refinement is not discrete. That conditioning is label-invariant but slightly
+  changes the random graph distribution.
