codex
The bare run ended `budget_bound`, not `too_easy`: every tested size was solved, but the harness explicitly says to park rather than reject because `escalate()` could still climb. The evidence also shows why—changing only \(n\) leaves a 12-step affine inverse equally visible. I’m using the remaining legitimate Track B axis now: replace each affine update with a bijective power-and-translation permutation over a prime field. This keeps the same paper-native false-twin witness and fixed two-element answer, but makes the compact inverse genuinely nontrivial while staying below the 300-operation cap.
