index 1a3dca334d3937d6087596c1ce86ae72f546d485..0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
--- a/README.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,159 +0,0 @@
-# False-twin certificates through a hidden coordinate change
-
-**Status: locally verified, but not yet shippable.** The mandatory OpenRouter
-calls all returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`, so STEP 4 and the oracle parts
-of G9 have no scored attempts. Provider errors are not evidence of hardness.
-
-| profile axis | declaration |
-|---|---|
-| track | `B` — no-tool compression; an efficient reference algorithm exists |
-| native domain | `combinatorics` |
-| object regime | `finite_discrete` |
-| computational core | `graph` |
-| certificate form | `integer_tuple` (a vertex pair) |
-| intended intuition | `change of variables` — reverse the modular coordinate chain |
-| domain essentiality | `native` |
-| reduction | none |
-
-## Problem and trust basis
-
-This generator turns Estrada-Moreno, Rodríguez-Velázquez, and Yero's
-[*The k-metric dimension of a graph*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6840) into an
-exact witness problem. The solver receives a succinct, complete adjacency rule
-for a simple connected graph and must return its false-twin pair. False twins
-are nonadjacent vertices with identical open neighborhoods. Only the two twins
-themselves distinguish that pair, while every pair in a connected graph is
-distinguished by its own two vertices; the returned pair therefore certifies
-that the largest feasible metric multiplicity is exactly 2. `verify` checks the
-two modular base coordinates exactly and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
-
-Generation is inverse. It first samples the internal path coordinate to be
-doubled, pushes it through a random chain of invertible updates
-`y <- ±2y+b (mod n-1)`, and publishes only the final target. A separate affine
-permutation relabels all graph vertices. The resulting graph is a path with one
-internal vertex replaced by two false twins, so the certificate is known before
-the instance is assembled.
-
-Section 1 supplies the exact definition. Theorem 2.2 identifies the maximum
-`k` as `min |D_G(x,y)|`, and Corollary 2.3 says that a connected graph is
-2-metric dimensional exactly when it contains twins. This corollary is also the
-easy-result warning: Track A would be false. On Track B, forward enumeration of
-the affine-chain preimage is the reference algorithm. It is
-`O(n * rounds)` and, on shipping seed 314159, scanned 868,376 coordinates and
-performed 31,261,542 modular operations in about 0.9 seconds. The compact route
-reverses 12 updates and maps two canonical coordinates to public labels, at
-most 78 exact arithmetic operations.
-
-## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
-
-The complete demo data are:
-
-```text
-n=12, m=11, A=7, B=4, target=10
-updates:
-  1: y <- 2y       (mod 11)
-  2: y <- 2y + 8   (mod 11)
-base(v)=f when x(v)=11, and x(v) otherwise,
-where x(v)=7^(-1)(v-4) mod 12 and f maps to target 10.
-Two vertices are adjacent iff their base coordinates differ by 1.
-```
-
-Working backward gives `f=6`. Its public label is `7*6+4 mod 12 = 10`;
-the exceptional canonical index 11 has label `7*11+4 mod 12 = 9`.
-Thus `<answer>[9, 10]</answer>` is the answer.
-`verify(inst, [9, 10])` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping one entry returns
-`(False, "wrong number of vertices: expected 2, got 1")`. This demo is
-comfortably hand-solvable.
-
-## Difficulty presets
-
-| preset | virtual vertices `n` | update rounds | answer length | status |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| `demo` | 12 | 2 | 2 | hand example only |
-| `easy` | 2,000,002 | 12 | 2 | proposed shipping level; local gates pass |
-| `medium` | 3,000,002 | 12 | 2 | construction verified; oracle not reached |
-| `hard` | 4,000,002 | 12 | 2 | construction verified; oracle not reached |
-
-`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is provisionally `easy`. Escalation grows only the
-virtual graph; the two-entry witness and 78-operation compact route stay fixed.
-
-## Gate results
-
-| gate | measured result |
-|---|---|
-| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verified (4 presets × 3 seeds) |
-| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
-| G3 | prose plus fenced JSON round-tripped both indices |
-| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact probability `1/2,000,001 = 4.9999975e-7` |
-| G5 | exact valid count 1; reference scan solved in about 0.9 s, 868,376 coordinates, 31,261,542 modular operations |
-| G6 | five attacks failed 0/8; the Track B reference algorithm solved 8/8 as expected |
-| G7 | doubled `n=4,000,004` instance verified with answer length still 2 |
-| G8 | 40/40 affine/reflection transformations invariant, 40/40 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
-| G9 | size/effort caps pass (16 chars, 4 tokens, 2 atoms, 78 operations); oracle diagnostic blocked by quota |
-
-The five failing G6 attacks were: pair the exceptional vertex with a degree-one
-endpoint, pair it with the smallest public label, 256 random partner restarts,
-treat the displayed target as its own preimage, and undo only the final update.
-The separate reference scan evaluated the full update chain on successive
-coordinates and solved every seed, which is expected and explicitly reported
-for Track B.
-
-## Oracle and G9 evidence
-
-No row below is a scored attempt. Each harness run exhausted its four error
-retries and stopped rather than treating an unreachable model as a failure.
-
-| arm | preset | solved / attempts | result |
-|---|---|---:|---|
-| bare | `easy` | 0 / 0 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
-| structural hint | `easy` | 0 / 0 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
-| placebo hint | `easy` | 0 / 0 | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
-
-Hinted minus placebo is therefore undefined, and nothing can yet be concluded
-about whether the change-of-variables hint helps. The error-only transcripts
-are retained as evidence of the blocked runs, not as hardness evidence.
-
-## Use
-
-The module uses only the Python standard library; `gvlib` is not required for
-this integer graph certificate.
-
-```python
-import gen_1312_6840 as gen
-
-params = gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
-inst = gen.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
-question = gen.render(inst)
-candidate = gen.parse_answer("<answer>[123, 456]</answer>")
-ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, candidate)
-```
-
-After restoring OpenRouter quota, run the bare loop from this directory and the
-hinted/placebo loops in isolated scratch directories, then refresh the report:
-
-```bash
-python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_1312_6840.py
-bash ../../scripts/emit.sh 1312.6840 20 easy
-```
-
-## Caveats
-
-- This is intentionally not a Track A claim. Corollary 2.3 and a linear
-  collision scan make the family easy with ordinary code; the intended
-  difficulty is recognizing and executing the short inverse chain without a
-  tool.
-- G4 conditions on the public image of canonical index `n-1`, because the
-  statement makes that member of the pair free to infer. Its probability is
-  only for a uniform guess of the remaining partner; it says nothing about a
-  solver that reverses some or all updates.
-- The compact-operation count treats each exact subtraction, reduction, sign
-  change, parity correction, division, multiplication, or addition as one
-  operation. Large-integer transcription risk remains even below the cap.
-- The graph is represented succinctly by a complete adjacency rule rather than
-  by two million explicit adjacency rows. This preserves the paper's native
-  graph object but makes representation-aware algebraic attacks relevant.
-- The panel did not try symbolic composition of all affine rounds into one map;
-  that is another successful compact method, not a failing attack, and belongs
-  to the intended Track B solution class.
-- Until the bare and hinted multi-vendor runs produce real attempts, this bundle
-  must not be submitted as hardened.
